.he "wall (1)                     9/30/26                     wall (1)"
.fo "                               -#-"
.in 5
.rm 65
.bd
NAME
.in +5
.br
.bd
wall
- write to all users
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SYNTAX
.in +5
.br
.bd
wall
.br
.bd
wall file
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
DESCRIPTION
.in +5
.br
.bd
Wall
reads the standard input up to an end of file, then sends this to the
terminal of each currently logged in user.
.sp
Its normal use is to inform users of immediate system status.  Only the
super-user may use
.bd
wall.
.sp
The users are those the file /etc/utmp names, and each is sent to the
terminal that file records for them.  A record with an empty user name
is a terminal nobody is logged in on and is passed over.  What arrives
at a terminal is headed
.bd
Broadcast message ...
and the text of the broadcast follows the heading.
.sp
A user who is logged in is written to one after another, a second
between them, and each is written to by a process of its own, so that a
terminal that will not take the message holds up neither the others nor
the shell that ran wall.
.sp
With a file named, the broadcast is read from that file instead of from
the standard input.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
EXAMPLE
.in +5
.br
Every user logged in is sent what is typed at
.bd
wall
until an end of file.  For
.sp
.in +5
.ti +5
wall
.br
System going down in 3 minutes -- Please prepare
.br
^D
.sp
.in -5
each user would then see
.bd
Broadcast message ...
followed by what appears above.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
NOTES
.in +5
.br
The 1982 page documents the command as
.bd
wall
with no arguments, and it is the page that says only the super-user may
use it.  The v7 source the port is taken from has no such test - in v7
any user could broadcast - so the check was added here to match the
page, and the refusal is "Not super-user" on the standard error, with
an exit status of 1.  The file argument is v7's and is kept; the page
does not mention it.
.sp
The page's example has each user seeing
.bd
Broadcast message ...
The v7 source capitalised the M.  The page's spelling is the one used.
.sp
The broadcast is read into a 3000 byte buffer and a longer one is cut
off there rather than written past the end of the buffer, which is what
the original did with it.
.sp
Wall writes to terminals and not to the people at them.  It is the mode
of the terminal that decides whether the open succeeds, and a terminal
that refuses the message simply receives nothing: the process that fails
is not wall's, and its complaint goes to a standard error nobody is
reading.
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FILES
.in +5
.br
/etc/utmp	(current user information)
.br
/dev/*		the terminals written to
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
SEE ALSO
.in +5
.br
who (1), write (1)
